# Copy to .env.local.
#
# The archive has no user accounts by design — reading and contributing need no
# login. There is exactly one maintainer account, and it gates /admin: the
# review queue, and everything that can write into data/people/.
#
# Unset, these fall back to admin / admin. That is fine on a laptop and
# unacceptable anywhere else — a production build (NODE_ENV=production)
# refuses to sign anyone in at all while these are unset, rather than leaving
# the default pair reachable from the internet. Set them before deploying.
ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me-to-something-long

# Signs the session cookie. Optional: left unset it is derived from the
# password above, which means changing the password signs everybody out. Set it
# explicitly if you would rather sessions survived a password change.
# ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET=some-long-random-string

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deployment (Netlify) — leave all of this unset for `npm run dev`
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# The public URL this deployment is reachable at. Used for canonical links,
# the sitemap, robots.txt and Open Graph tags — get this wrong and search
# engines index the wrong domain. Falls back to Netlify's own deploy URL if
# unset, which is fine for a preview but not for the real domain.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=https://vanshvriksha.srinidhibhat.com

# A fine-grained GitHub personal access token, scoped to *only* this
# repository, with the "Contents" permission set to Read and write. Without
# it, admin writes (approving a submission, editing a person, confirming a
# vocabulary entry) fall back to writing straight to the local filesystem —
# correct for `npm run dev`, silently data-losing on Netlify, whose function
# filesystem is wiped on every cold start. With it set, those same writes
# become commits to this repository instead (see src/lib/github.ts), which is
# what actually makes them durable in a deployment, and keeps the archive's
# stated design promise: every change to every record has an author and a
# reason in git history, not just a database row that was quietly edited.
#
# Create one at github.com/settings/personal-access-tokens/new → Repository
# access: "Only select repositories" → this repo → Permissions → Contents:
# Read and write. Nothing else needs granting.
# GITHUB_TOKEN=github_pat_...

# "owner/repo". Only needed if this deployment's repo is not
# srinidhibhat45/vamsha-vruksha (a fork, a rename, a different remote).
# GITHUB_REPO=srinidhibhat45/vamsha-vruksha

# The branch admin writes are committed to. Defaults to "main".
# GITHUB_BRANCH=main

# Netlify Blobs holds the moderation queue (data/submissions/) and
# contributed photographs (data/media/), for the same reason GITHUB_TOKEN
# exists above — Netlify Functions cannot write to their own filesystem and
# expect it to still be there on the next request. On Netlify itself these
# credentials are ambient and nothing below needs setting. They only need
# supplying outside a Netlify Function — a script, a self-hosted build.
# NETLIFY_SITE_ID=
# NETLIFY_BLOBS_TOKEN=
